## Finding

The Step 9 confirmation template renders the preview job UUID as R-46's stable
apply nonce:

```html
<input type="hidden" name="nonce" value="{{ view.job_id }}">
```

That is the accepted semantic: every confirmation of one completed preview — a
double-click, retry, back-and-resubmit, or second browser — must carry the same
request identity and converge on one apply.

The direct R-46 route currently does this instead:

```python
nonce = (form.get("nonce") or "").strip()
if not nonce:
    return _validation(request, "nonce")
```

It therefore accepts arbitrary, malformed, duplicated, padded, oversized, or
alternate nonces. A direct caller can generate different request keys for the
same preview even though the rendered Step 9 contract defines one canonical
identity. PostgreSQL's one-live-apply and applied-input constraints protect the
durable effect, but they do not make the request boundary honest or bounded.

